AppCall licensing boundaries

CORE
The current first-party Rust crates, Bun connector runtime, connectors,
tests, and build sources are licensed under AGPL-3.0-only. LICENSE contains
the unmodified license; NOTICE supplies preserved Meosu attribution.
Third-party material retains its own notices and applicable terms.

ENTERPRISE
No enterprise implementation is included in this release. Future modules
may be published in enterprise/ under a separate source-available commercial
license. That directory is blocked by the public release gate until its
exact files and license are reviewed. Visibility will not imply permission
to use or redistribute those modules under AGPL.

Candidates for future commercial modules include organization provisioning
and policy administration, managed deployment fleet administration, and
compliance reporting integrations. Core authentication, tenant isolation,
credential protection, workflow recovery, and ordinary connector execution
remain core. Existing preview branding and delegated billing screens are
not represented as production enterprise features.

A directory boundary alone does not resolve licensing of a combined program.
Any enterprise module linked with AGPL code requires a compatible grant or
separate commercial rights to all relevant code. Contributor agreements
must establish any rights needed for commercial relicensing; a contribution
sign-off alone does not provide those rights. No blanket linking exception
or commercial license to the core is granted by this document.

THIRD PARTIES
Anusa server software is an external service and is not included or licensed
by this repository. The retired Anusa Go SDK snapshot carried an MIT license;
its retained notice does not license the proprietary server. Rust integration
code and synthetic compatibility tests are distinct from Anusa server source.
Other dependencies and redistributed tools retain their own licenses. A
proprietary vendor binary must not be shipped merely because AppCall has an
enterprise license.

PUBLICATION
Publish only the reviewed export produced by scripts/public-release.py,
with fresh Git history. Internal notes, agent instructions, development
history, and extra Markdown files are excluded. Required third-party legal
notices are retained. Unresolved legal placeholders block export.
